From b0bddd0a4f92a13b2f30802f83f462ab75bbb155 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@lccc>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 10:10:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] linker: add relocation field table and checked writers
 (x86-64)

Every relocation writes a value into a fixed-width field. Upstream derives the
width and signedness at each of ~46 application sites, and only 8 of them carry a
range predicate: emit_exec.rs checks R_X86_64_PC32 while emit_shared.rs masks the
same relocation, so 'lccc-ld -shared' truncated an out-of-range displacement and
exited 0 where GNU ld reports 'relocation truncated to fit'.

State the ABI facts once, in a table, and give the write sites a checked writer
that consults it:

  - linker_common::reloc_field: RelocField{bits,signed}, fits(), bounds(), and
    the shared diagnostics. Shared because the invariant is not x86-specific.
  - x86::linker::reloc_field: the x86-64 field table (38 types, numbers verified
    against /usr/include/elf.h), name(), field(), is_no_check(), check(),
    check_width(), check_known(), bounds(), and w{8,16,32,64}_checked().

Design points:
  - A 64-bit field written from a 64-bit value cannot lose bits, so the check is
    vacuous there and is defined to pass. Refusing those would reject inputs GNU
    ld accepts (symbol at 0 with a negative addend).
  - bounds() uses checked_add: 'off + width <= len' both silently skips the write
    (success for an image missing a patch) and wraps for an offset near
    usize::MAX, after which the comparison passes and the slice index panics.
  - An unrecognised type is NOT exempt: it is checked against the writer's own
    width, with a diagnostic that says the signedness was inferred and points at
    the missing table entry rather than at -mcmodel=large.

22 unit tests, including the two that caught real defects while this was being
written: the unknown-type fail-open and the misleading signedness claim.
