From 817ddb152a1bc94312adf3deecaa709d523f0343 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@lccc>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 10:20:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] linker: wire the field table into all 32 x86-64 write
 sites; validate r_offset

Two defects, both measured before and after rather than argued.

1. Silent truncation. 46 relocation application sites existed and 8 carried a
   range predicate. emit_exec.rs checked R_X86_64_PC32 while emit_shared.rs masked
   the same relocation, so 'lccc-ld -shared' truncated an out-of-range
   displacement, wrote the wrong pointer and exited 0. All 32 unchecked field
   writes now go through w{8,16,32}_checked(), which takes the field from the ABI
   table instead of from the width of the store. The audit gate
   (tools/reloc_field_audit.py) reports SILENT-TRUNCATION GAPS: 0.

2. An unvalidated r_offset, which is worse than a crash. r_offset comes from the
   input object and is used to compute an index into the output image. Measured on
   a crafted object:

     r_offset = 2^64-16  the u64 sum wrapped, the unchecked writer no-oped, and
                         the image kept the unrelocated instruction: 'lea' loaded
                         the address of the next instruction instead of the
                         symbol's. Exit status 0.
     r_offset = size     the write landed four bytes into whatever the layout put
                         after the section. Exit status 0.

   GNU ld refuses both (bfd_reloc_outofrange, 'error 4'). A sweep of r_offset
   across the boundary, differential against GNU ld, established bfd's exact rule:
   offset + field width <= section size (with .text of 9 bytes and a 4-byte field,
   5 is accepted and 6 is refused). Two layers implement it:

     - parse_object.rs rejects r_offset >= section size, and an out-of-range
       sh_info, which used to make a whole relocation table vanish silently. This
       is architecture-independent, so x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V all inherit it.
     - reloc_field::offset_in_section() applies the width-aware half at the three
       x86-64 application loops, because the field width is arch-specific.

   Verified by sweeping every offset from 0 to size+3 for both a 4-byte and an
   8-byte field and comparing the accept/refuse decision against GNU ld: 25
   comparisons, 0 divergences, with the two boundaries differing exactly as the
   field widths require.

Loop-invariant data (section size and name, object name) is hoisted out of the
per-relocation loop, so the check costs one comparison and one jump-table lookup
per relocation and no re-indexing.

27 unit tests for the two reloc_field modules, including the two that caught real
defects while they were being written.
