From 550e0af5b3b75e2a50075f0db6c05cf7fcd6df32 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@lccc>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 10:31:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] linker+asm: fix six wrong relocation numbers; make the
 offset check ELF-class aware

Found by tools/reloc_number_audit.py, which compares every R_* constant defined
in the sources against <elf.h>. A wrong relocation number cannot be spotted by
reading code -- the wrong number looks exactly like the right one -- and nothing
downstream can notice, because the number never reaches the output.

AArch64, arm/linker/reloc.rs: the five TLSLE_MOVW_TPREL types were numbered in
ascending name order (G0=544 ... G2=548). <elf.h> numbers them by descending
significance (G2=544 ... G0_NC=548). The arm bodies are keyed by name and are
correct, so the rotation made the *numbers* select the wrong arm: a real G0 (547)
was encoded with 'tp >> 16' instead of 'tp & 0xffff', and a real G0_NC (548) with
'tp >> 32'. Four of the five types were mis-encoded; only G1 escaped, and only
because the arm it landed in uses the same shift. The _NC suffix is what made it
silent -- the ABI exempts those forms from range checking, so a wrong TLS offset
produced no diagnostic. Three tests pin the numbers and the per-type slice; two
of the three fail when the rotation is restored (verified by mutation).

i686 assembler encoder: R_386_TLS_LE_32 was 37, which is R_386_TLS_TPOFF32, so
objects written by this assembler disagreed with the i686 linker's own table
(linker/types.rs has the correct 34). The @TPOFF modifier was emitted as a
constant named R_386_32S with value 38 -- there is no R_386_32S in the i386 ABI
(that is an x86-64 type), and 38 is R_386_SIZE32, so a TLS offset was written as
a symbol-size relocation. Both corrected to what GAS emits, measured on the same
source: @NTPOFF -> R_386_TLS_LE (17), @TPOFF -> R_386_TLS_LE_32 (34). The
invented name is deleted rather than renumbered.

emit_script.rs: this file links ELF32/i386 and ELF64/x86-64 through one
machine-parameterised function, so the new offset check has to pick its table by
ELF class. Type 1 is R_X86_64_64 (8 bytes) and R_386_32 (4 bytes); consulting the
x86-64 table for an i386 object made the check report a 4-byte section as too
small for its own 4-byte relocation, and three i386 script tests failed. Only
names and widths are taken from an i386 table, not signedness: the i386 arms
already carry range checks whose semantics are documented per type (R_386_32
accepts the union of both 32-bit ranges; R_386_PC16 is deliberately unchecked
because ELF i386 defines it modulo 2^16 and Linux real-mode code relies on that).

Also: LINKER_DEBUG_TLS was read with std::env::var inside the relocation arms --
a lock, an environment scan and a String allocation per TLS relocation, paid on
every link. It cannot change during a link, so it is now read once through a
OnceLock.

linker suite 163/0/0, lib tests 2740/0.
