From 3f97839034239ecc591ca0a1c5c6c79f4898bd26 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@lccc>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 11:04:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] linker: implement --defsym properly on all three x86
 paths; add R_X86_64_16/8

Five defects, all found by tests that assert against GNU ld rather than against
my own expectations.

1. --defsym handled only the alias form, and failed silently.
   link_builtin and link_shared each had `if let Some(t) = globals.get(target)`,
   so a constant (`--defsym far=0x1000`) or an expression missed the lookup and
   produced no definition at all. The link then failed with "undefined symbols:
   far" -- blaming the reference for a definition the user had just given. The
   script path ignored --defsym entirely.

   Classification now lives in one place, linker_common/defsym, so the three
   paths cannot disagree about what a right-hand side means: Alias copies a
   definition, Constant becomes an absolute symbol, Expression is evaluated
   against symbols known at that point. Number spelling was measured against
   /usr/bin/ld, not assumed: 1G/1T/0b1011/0o17/0777 accepted, "(1", "1+", "1 2"
   syntax errors, "1/0" reported as "/ by zero" and not as a typo, scale suffixes
   1024-based. An expression on the script path that names a symbol living inside
   an output section cannot be evaluated before layout, and says so
   (NeedsLayout) instead of guessing.

   lccc-ld also only recognised `--defsym=`; the two-argument `--defsym SYM=VAL`
   form GNU ld accepts fell through to "ignoring unknown option" and the
   expression was then consumed as an input filename, reporting
   "failed to read 'far=0x1000'".

2. R_X86_64_16 (12) and R_X86_64_8 (14) were unimplemented on the executable and
   shared paths -- "unsupported x86-64 relocation type 12" for objects that the
   relocation table already described (field() had returned U16/U8 for them, with
   unit tests). Both now go through w16_checked/w8_checked, so the range check
   comes from the same table entry as the store and an unsigned 0xffff is
   accepted rather than rejected by a signed test. The script path already had
   them; all three now agree.

3. Two fail-open guards removed: emit_script wrapped w8_checked in
   `if fp < out.len()`, which converts the writer's bounds refusal into a silent
   success -- a link that reports an object it never patched.

4. Script-path range diagnostics now use one shape, GNU ld's
   ("relocation truncated to fit: R_X86_64_PC32 against symbol 'farpc'"), and the
   type name is derived from the relocation tables (reloc_field::name for ELF64,
   i386_field for ELF32). It previously said "32-bit PC-relative reference ...
   does not fit", which named no type; hardcoding R_X86_64_PC32 would have named
   the wrong ABI on the i386 path this same checker serves.

5. Removed a local `const STT_NOTYPE_: u8 = 0` duplicating
   crate::backend::elf::STT_NOTYPE.

Tests: 12 unit tests for the classifier/evaluator (including the overflow,
division-by-zero, wrapping-difference and 1024-not-1000 cases), plus the linker
suite's reloc tag extended with r_offset sweeps differentially compared against
GNU ld and per-field-width range tests that fail if lccc is either laxer or
stricter than bfd. Linker suite 178 pass / 0 fail / 0 warn (was 163 pass, 11 of
the new cases failing); lib tests 2746 passed / 0 failed; zero build warnings.
---
 src/backend/linker_common/defsym.rs   | 538 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 src/backend/linker_common/mod.rs      |   1 +
 src/backend/x86/linker/emit_exec.rs   |  28 +-
 src/backend/x86/linker/emit_script.rs | 197 ++++++++--
