From 8a1005d5ed0e522b87d1e039db95b3bf15a439f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@lccc>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 11:28:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] linker/defsym: match bfd's number grammar exactly,
 measured value by value

Rewrote the numeric scanner against measurements rather than assumptions, taken
by linking with /usr/bin/ld 2.44 (GNU Binutils for Debian) and reading each
symbol's value back out of the output symbol table:

    1K -> 0x400          1m -> 0x100000        0x10K -> 0x4000
    0777M -> 0x1ff00000  1025K -> 0x100400
    4G, 1T, 1P, 1E, 1Z -> syntax error         0b1011K, 0o17K -> syntax error
    18446744073709551616, 0x10000000000000000,
    99999999999999999999999999 -> 0xffffffffffffffff

Three corrections follow from that:

1. Scale suffixes are K and M only. The first version accepted G, T and P, which
   made lccc-ld laxer than the linker it replaces -- the dangerous direction,
   because the build links here and is refused by GNU ld on someone else's
   machine. The suffix also applies after the radix scan (0x10K is 16 KiB) and
   is rejected on explicitly prefixed 0b/0o literals, both as bfd does.

2. Overflow saturates at u64::MAX instead of being rejected. Rejecting was
   stricter than bfd; wrapping would have placed the symbol at 0, a plausible
   address and therefore a silent miscompile. Saturation is the only one of the
   three that is neither, and it is what bfd does.

3. The suffix must come off *before* the radix is detected. Detecting the radix
   first read 0777M as decimal 777 MiB (0x30900000) instead of octal 511 MiB
   (0x1ff00000) -- not an error, a silently wrong address. This one was caught
   by the test that records bfd's measured value rather than a value computed
   from the implementation, which is the only reason it was caught at all.

Also: -nostdlib, -nostartfiles and -nodefaultlibs are now benign no-ops in
lccc-ld instead of "ignoring unknown option" warnings. A standalone linker never
adds default libraries -- that is the driver's job -- so these have no effect
here, and warning about them on every kernel-style link is noise that hides the
warnings which matter.

Verified end to end against GNU ld on both front ends: constants, suffixes,
radix spellings, saturation, arithmetic expressions (s3=0x1000+1K -> 0x1400) and
aliases (astart=_start resolves to each linker's own _start) all produce
identical values, and malformed input is refused by both.

Linker suite 178 pass / 0 fail / 0 warn; lib tests 2746 passed / 0 failed
(12 defsym unit tests); zero build warnings; cargo fmt clean.
